<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyrenaica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyrenaica is the eastern coastal region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, named after the ancient Greek city of Cyrene. It lay on the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. Under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> extended its control over Cyrenaica.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrenaica.jpg" alt="Cyrenaica" width="316" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esemono; derivative work by Felitsata · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Cyrenaica_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cyrenaica rests on a mass of Miocene limestone that tilts up steeply from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The Jebel Akhdar extends parallel to the coast from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-sirte">Gulf of Sidra</a> to the Gulf of Bomba and reaches 882 metres, with a Mediterranean climate, and the red soils of the Marj Plain have borne wheat and barley from ancient times. To the south and east the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> reaches the coast in the region known in antiquity as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marmarica">Marmarica</a>. The coastal region was called the Pentapolis, or Five Cities, in antiquity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> began to colonise Cyrenaica in the seventh century BCE. Its first and most important colony, Cyrene, was established about 631 BCE by settlers from the island of Thera, who had left it because of a severe famine. The five cities were Cyrene with its port of Apollonia, Arsinoe, Euesperides, Balagrae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-marj">Barce</a>. The region produced grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, wine and wool, and silphium, a herb that grew only there. Cyrene became a great intellectual centre, home of the Cyrenaics founded by Aristippus and of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a>. Persian forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> seized the Pentapolis in 525 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> annexed it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-apion">Ptolemy Apion</a>, dying without heirs in 96 BCE, bequeathed it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>.</p><p>The name Cyrenaica dates to the first century BCE. It became a senatorial province in 20 BCE and was split in 293 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> into Libya Pentapolis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya-inferior">Libya Sicca</a>. Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> conquered the region about 642, and it became known as Barqah after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-marj">Barce</a>. In the middle of the 11th century Arab tribes including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> ravaged it, and up to 200,000 Hilalian families migrated into it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. It later fell under Ottoman Libya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cyrenaica is the eastern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, between the 16th and 25th meridians east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> occupied it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> in 1911 and made it a colony in 1919, and on 1 January 1934 Tripolitania, Cyrenaica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> were united as Italian Libya. In 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-of-libya">Idris al-Senussi</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Cyrenaica, which joined the Kingdom of Libya, independent from 24 December 1951, with Idris as king. The Senussi dynasty was overthrown on 1 September 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cyrenaica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibrahim's charge covered what is now eastern Algeria, Tunisia and Tripolitania, and in theory the whole Maghreb west of <b>Cyrenaica</b>, since the territory granted him was never demarcated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Darius I most of the people of Barca in <b>Cyrenaica</b> were deported there, and under Xerxes I the descendants of Greek priests from near Didyma were forcibly relocated to the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It is marked by the internment of much of <b>Cyrenaica</b>'s population in camps and the execution of the resistance leader Omar Mukhtar in 1931, and by the massacre in Addis Ababa that followed the attempt on Viceroy Rodolfo Graziani's life in February 1937.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Ptolemies strengthened their control through military power and diplomatic alliances, and by the reign of Ptolemy II Philadelphus (r. 283–246 BCE) they also ruled <b>Cyrenaica</b>, Cyprus, southern Syria and parts of the Aegean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They destroyed the Sasanian Empire, ended Byzantine power permanently in Syria, Palestine, Egypt and <b>Cyrenaica</b>, and extended Arab authority from Libya to the frontiers of Central Asia and the borders of Sind, all within about a quarter of a century and by a society with no prior tradition…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In Libya Fascist Italy waged a campaign of mass killings, concentration camps and the forced starvation of thousands, and expelled 100,000 Bedouin Cyrenaicans, half the population of <b>Cyrenaica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
